From: EglantinePS5 <Vampinity@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] Hot pages stay untrapped (EDEN_UP_HOT_PAGES)

EDEN_UP_HOT_PAGES=1 (exact 1, off without it; Frizti/Fritchii d20c32704c, there EDEN_NX2_EXP48,
a port of Eglantine f27522bfb9 by kraibse). A buffer page the CPU rewrites again and again is no
longer write-trapped after each upload: it uploads once a frame whether it changed or not, and the
trap, the gather and the re-protect of every first write to it are saved. Upstream: 71% fewer
protect calls and 5% of frame time on a PC.

- A page becomes hot when a trapped write finds it re-protected twice inside a 128-frame window;
  a page the GPU wrote never does. Hot state is kept per 4 MiB region and made with the region.
- It refuses to act with the copy guard on (EDEN_UP_AHEAD_GUARD), and says so in the log: the
  guard's ALL_WRITES path relies on the cache's trap for pages outside its guessed set. So this
  is for the full frame wait ("clean" mode), not for fast mode.
- The switch is read once, in the memory tracker's constructor, into a plain flag: the readers
  (CpuWriteEpoch per draw, ChangeRegionState, ForEachModifiedRange) do one load with it off.
- From our review, on top of the draft: the per-draw sync memo keeps working inside a frame
  (CpuWriteEpoch adds the frame number for regions with hot state, instead of returning "always
  changed"); the bank-flag recheck no longer takes "modified" for "uploaded by this bind"; the
  state is allocated with the region, not inside noexcept functions.

The price upstream names, to be checked on video: a write to a hot page AFTER its upload of the
frame reaches the GPU one frame late (two draws' uniform blocks in one 4 KiB page): a one-frame
jitter of one object, a particle or a HUD element.

Drafted with an AI tool and reviewed (off path compared with the old code on 20,000 random
tracker operations with 5 seeds: identical; buffer_cache.h's 7 anchors unchanged).

